      <h2>The 5 Operational Headaches of Tracking AIFs</h2>
      <p>
        If you have ever attempted to track an AIF in a standard retail portfolio tracker (like Zerodha Kite, Groww, or INDmoney), you already know the frustration: they simply do not support them. Even manual spreadsheets break down because AIF mechanics are fundamentally different from standard listed stocks:
      </p>

      <ol style="line-height: 1.8;">
        <li>
          <strong>Committed Capital vs Drawn Capital:</strong> In a ₹1 Crore AIF commitment, you do not transfer ₹1 Crore upfront. Instead, the fund manager issues periodic "Capital Calls" (e.g. 25% in Year 1, 35% in Year 2). Tracking your portfolio requires distinguishing between your <em>Total Commitment</em>, <em>Cumulative Drawn Capital</em>, and <em>Uncalled Commitment</em>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Return of Capital vs Capital Gains Distributions:</strong> When the fund exits an asset, the cash sent to your bank account often consists of a return of principal (reducing your cost basis) combined with profit distributions. Recording this as a simple dividend or redemption distorts your tax reporting and returns.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Infrequent Valuation Marks:</strong> Unlike stocks that tick every second, Category I and II AIFs publish Net Asset Values (NAVs) only quarterly or semi-annually after valuation audits. A robust portfolio tracker must maintain historical valuation snapshots without throwing errors between report dates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hurdle Rates &amp; Carried Interest:</strong> Most AIFs operate on an 8% to 10% Hurdle Rate. The GP (General Partner) only collects performance fees (typically 15% to 20% carry) once your hurdle rate is fulfilled. Net returns depend directly on whether fees are deducted pre- or post-NAV.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Multi-PAN Family Allocations:</strong> High-net-worth families frequently split commitments across Self, Spouse, and family trusts or HUF accounts to optimize capital gains exemptions. Juggling separate PDF statements across different entities makes household wealth visibility almost impossible.
        </li>
      </ol>

